% A page larger than the device can hold.
%
% A device that keeps its page as one block of memory reaches a pixel by
% its position within that block, and that position is counted in the
% width the platform expresses the size of a block in. So what a device
% can hold is settled by two things and not one: whether the block's far
% end has an address here at all, and whether the machine will give the
% memory for it. The first is a limit of the implementation and no amount
% of memory lifts it; the second is a limit of the machine and a larger
% machine lifts it. PLRM 8.2 has a name for each -- limitcheck for the
% first, VMerror for the second -- and a page that fails either is
% refused before any of it is built.
%
% Refusing before building is what keeps a page nobody can hold from
% asking the system for the memory to hold it, and it is why the
% interpreter is still standing afterwards. The page size is a program's
% to choose, so this is reachable by any program, and an interpreter that
% runs on afterwards is the point of the refusal: what follows must still
% draw. PLRM 3.11 has the error machinery exist precisely so a program may
% handle an error and continue, and a refusal a program cannot carry on
% from is not an error but a stop.
%
% What this holds is the refusal and the carrying on, which read the same
% on every build. Which of the two limits a given page meets does not:
% the ceiling a size expresses is the platform's own, and the same page
% is past a narrow build's ceiling and inside a wide one's. That question
% belongs to the arithmetic and is held there, against each platform's
% own boundary, by tests/raster_extent_test.c. The page asked for below
% is past every machine's memory and past a narrow build's ceiling
% besides, so it is refused on all of them and costs nothing anywhere.

/failures 0 def
/fail { /failures failures 1 add store (FAIL: ) print print (\n) print flush }
bind def
/assert { exch { pop }{ fail } ifelse } bind def

% Asked of a device that keeps its raster as one block indexed by a
% pixel's position. A device whose page is built some other way has no
% such index and no such limit, and answers about a page this size on
% its own terms: one holding its rows as an array of strings says so by
% carrying ImgData, one emitting a stream of drawing operators says so
% by drawing its glyphs as outlines rather than pixels, and one that
% rasterizes nothing at all says so by declaring it -- which is what a
% device holding the page as the marks that made it declares, having no
% pixels to index until it comes to put the page out.
/indexed? DEVICE /ImgData known not
          DEVICE /VectorGlyphs known not and
          DEVICE /NoOutput known not and def

% Two thousand million on a side. Nothing is allocated to find out: at
% any pixel size at all the block runs past every machine's memory, and
% the refusal is reached by arithmetic before the first byte is asked
% for, so this costs the same as a page of no size whatever.
/refused false def
/why null def
{ << /PageSize [2000000000 2000000000] >> setpagedevice } stopped
{ /refused true store /why $error /errorname get store } if
clear

indexed?
{
    refused (a page larger than the device can hold is refused) assert
    refused
    { why /VMerror eq  why /limitcheck eq  or
      (the refusal names a limit of the machine or of the implementation)
      assert } if
}
{
    (# this device does not index one block of pixels: a page of that size
   is its own question\n) print flush
} ifelse

% ---- the shape a page's geometry is stated in

% PLRM 6.2 Table 6.1 gives PageSize, HWResolution and PageOffset as two
% numbers each and ImagingBBox as four or null. A value of another shape
% is not a page this interpreter can make: read where the page is set up
% it is indexed rather than refused -- a string indexes as the bytes it
% holds -- so a request naming a page of (abc) would be a page of 97 by
% 98 points reported back as the page that was asked for. Each is refused
% as the request is offered, before anything of the page is built, and
% names the entry it could not take in $error (PLRM 3.11).

/shaperr null def
/sbase 0 def
/refusal {                              % key value  .  name|null
    /shaperr null store
    1 dict dup 4 2 roll put             % dict
    count 1 sub /sbase exch store
    { setpagedevice } stopped
    { /shaperr $error /errorname get store } if
    % the request is the program's and a refusal may leave it standing:
    % come back to the depth the call was made at rather than clearing,
    % which would take the verdict with it
    count sbase sub dup 0 gt { { pop } repeat }{ pop } ifelse
    shaperr
} bind def
/infoat {                               % index  .  any|null
    $error /errorinfo get dup type /arraytype ne { pop pop null }{
        exch 2 copy exch length lt { get }{ pop pop null } ifelse
    } ifelse
} bind def

/PageSize (abc) refusal /typecheck eq
    (a page size that is not an array is refused) assert
/PageSize [612 792 1] refusal /typecheck eq
    (a page size of three numbers is refused) assert
/PageSize [612] refusal /typecheck eq
    (a page size of one number is refused) assert
/PageSize [612 (x)] refusal /typecheck eq
    (a page size whose element is not a number is refused) assert
0 infoat /PageSize eq
    (the refusal names the entry it could not take) assert
/HWResolution [72 72 72] refusal /typecheck eq
    (a resolution of three numbers is refused) assert
/PageOffset [0 0 0] refusal /typecheck eq
    (a page offset of three numbers is refused) assert
/ImagingBBox [0 0 100] refusal /typecheck eq
    (an imaging box of three numbers is refused) assert

% and the shapes the specification does give are taken
/PageSize [612 792] refusal null eq
    (a page size of two numbers is accepted) assert
/PageSize [612.5 792.25] refusal null eq
    (a page size of two reals is accepted) assert
/HWResolution [72 72] refusal null eq
    (a resolution of two numbers is accepted) assert
/PageOffset [0 0] refusal null eq
    (a page offset of two numbers is accepted) assert
/ImagingBBox [0 0 100 100] refusal null eq
    (an imaging box of four numbers is accepted) assert
/ImagingBBox null refusal null eq
    (an imaging box of null is accepted) assert

% The interpreter carries on. This is asked of every device, whatever it
% made of the page above: a device that refused it spent nothing, a
% device that priced it refused before building any of it, and a device
% that accepted it drew it. None of those is a reason the next job
% cannot run.
/drew false def
{
    << /PageSize [200 200] >> setpagedevice
    0 setgray newpath 10 10 moveto 100 100 lineto stroke
    /drew true store
} stopped { (a job after the refusal did not run) fail } if
clear
drew (a job after the refusal draws) assert

failures 0 eq { (SUCCESS) = } { (FAILURES: ) print failures = } ifelse
